.SH A) OVERVIEW
conditions are technically unnecessary as they simply provide small break-points
in testing steps.  they are like paragraphs, not manditory, but very useful
for providing obvious structure, grouping, and navigation.  one key use is to
identify repeating code as discussed under SEQUENCING and DITTO.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
conditions are translated as block of code within scripts and provide their
own statistics to help guild testers to the problem.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
conditions are very simple with only two fields.  the leading spaces are changed
to spacers (·) in this manual for clarity.

   verb------ § ---description----------- §
   ···COND    § verify function call      §

.SH D) VERB
COND is the verb and is expected to be indented three (3) characters off-estting
it from SCRP and later steps.  the verb field are is twelve (12) characters,
overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb------ § ---description----------- §
   ··· COND   § ·                         §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of sixty-five (65) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

   verb------ § ---description----------- §
   ···COND    § verify function call      §


.SH F) COND OUTPUT
when the unit-test is run, two (2) specific lines are generated for each
condition -- one before its steps and the other after.

when a condition is reached, the output will indicate a condition start,
that it is the 16th in the current script, and that the source data is on
line 452 of the unit test script.  the line mumber is very, very useful.

   ···COND [016] verify function call -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -[00452]

when all steps in a condition are complete, a footer appears with condition
specific statistics.  DNOC is COND backward ;) the footer says their where
4 tests and 4 passed with zero failures.  this line will also be "green"
if all passed or "red" with any failed for fast visual scanning.

   ······DNOC  ----------------- test=4  [ pass=4  fail=0  badd=0  void=0  ]

.SH G) SEQUENCING
every script can have up to ten (01) reusable conditions, meaning reexection
including all the steps under the condition.  the sequence markers are simply
a open paren followed by [0-9] and a closed paren right after the COND verb.

   verb--------- § ---description----------- §
   ···COND  (3)  § verify function call      §

additionally, sequence numbers can be overwritten in the same script for
more flexibility.

.SH H) DITTO
COND reuse is so simple it will be covered here. to repeat a condition, simply
use the DITTO verb with which every COND sequence number you want.  the
DITTO only has one field.

   verb--------- §
   ···DITTO (3)  §

.SH I) DITTO OUTPUT
formatted exactly like the COND output, the DITTO output starts with DOND vs
COND, labels it as the 31st condition executed in the current script, and
that the ditto appears in the source file at 552.  it also show the DITTO
sequence number (3) near the beginning.

   ···DOND [031] (3) [[ verify function call ]]- -- -- -- -- -- -- -[00552]

the summary line is exactly the same.

   ······DNOC  ----------------- test=4  [ pass=4  fail=0  badd=0  void=0  ]

NOTE: if the line number is [00555] is is in a specific unit test source file.
if like å00555æ it is in a shared file -- unit_data.unit or unit_wide.unit.
